From 7197dd4244e350d96aaf355321d8997f168b7d97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xalpharush <0xalpharush@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] msan: a sealed start-up
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Sealed init (patch 0012) made the common runtime read no environment and no
/proc, but MSan's own start-up still took paths that break the sealed contract:
it installed its signal handlers, a detection exited with a status the program
could have returned rather than dying by a signal, it re-execed on an unlimited
stack, and it mapped its fixed layout with MAP_FIXED over whatever was already
there.  This gives MSan the sealed start-up ASan already has, mechanism for
mechanism, in MSan's own code: sharing asan's would rebuild the asan runtime
and move the archive md5 the OSS-Fuzz proofs record.

Layout reservation (msan.cpp, msan_linux.cpp).  Unsealed MSan dry-runs the
layout with MemoryRangeIsAvailable, re-execs if it conflicts, then MAP_FIXEDs
its shadow.  In sealed mode MemoryRangeIsAvailable answers yes unasked (0012),
so the dry run cannot fail and the MAP_FIXED covers whatever the program or
kernel had already placed in a shadow range -- a placement the specification
these binaries are verified against admits.  SealedReserveShadow instead
reserves every SHADOW and mapped-ORIGIN range readable-writable and every
INVALID and unmapped-ORIGIN range PROT_NONE, each with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, and
spins on refusal via the common SealedHangOnAllocationFailure -- the shape of
0019's SealedReserveShadow for ASan.  The map/protect rule is one helper,
LayoutRangeAction, which InitShadow now uses too, so the two cannot disagree.

Two things differ from asan and both matter:

  - The ALLOCATOR range is reserved as well, PROT_NONE.  MSan's primary
    allocator has a fixed base (msan_allocator.cpp: 0x700000000000 on x86_64,
    0xE00000000000 on aarch64), which it maps with MAP_FIXED; asan's kernel
    picks its base.  Left open, a mapping the kernel placed in that range was
    silently replaced: the runtime's own first pages, or the [vdso] and [vvar].
    Reserved, such a mapping is a refusal (the spin), and the allocator's
    MAP_FIXED lands on the runtime's own reservation.

  - The reservation is made at the top of __msan_init, right after the frame is
    recorded and before AvoidCVE_2016_2143(), as AsanInitInternal reserves
    asan's shadow before the flag parser takes the runtime's first page.  It
    used to be made inside InitShadowWithReExec, after CacheBinaryName(),
    InitializeFlags() and InitializeInterceptors() had already made unhinted
    mappings -- which the kernel could put inside the allocator range.
    __msan_get_track_origins() reads a link-time symbol, not a flag, so it is
    known there.  The later InitShadowWithReExec call finds the layout reserved
    and does nothing.

SealFlags (msan.cpp).  ASan pins a set of sanitizer-neutral common flags in
asan_flags.cpp's SealFlags(); MSan had none.  The same common flags are pinned
at the end of InitializeFlags, after __msan_default_options() and MSAN_OPTIONS
are parsed so a program hook cannot reset them: handle_* = kHandleSignalNo and
use_sigaltstack off (no signal handler); abort_on_error on (a detection dies by
SIGABRT, not an exit status the program could have returned); hard/soft
rss_limit_mb 0 (no RSS-limit thread); heap_profile, detect_leaks off;
allocator_release_to_os_interval_ms -1; and clear_shadow_mmap_threshold at its
maximum, for asan's reason: above that threshold MSan's SetShadow zeroes a
shadow range by mapping fresh pages over it with MAP_FIXED, which in the model
is a new grant carved out of the shadow mapping, while the madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)
patch 0020 allows must lie inside one grant.  asan also pins
protect_shadow_gap, which MSan does not have.  Three flags asan's seal leaves
alone are pinned too, all already off by default, so a hook cannot turn on a
syscall the seal rules out: compress_stack_depot, print_module_map,
decorate_proc_maps.  The values are duplicated from asan's seal rather than
shared, on purpose.

ReExec (msan.cpp).  MSan also re-execs when the stack limit is unlimited.  In
sealed mode GetArgsAndEnv returns empty, so that ReExec would run /proc/self/exe
with an empty argv and environment; it is gated off, as the dry-run re-exec is.
With an unlimited stack the kernel's legacy bottom-up layout can start inside
MSan's shadow, and the reservation then refuses: the sealed spin, which
README.md's "Known limitation" describes.

Measured, x86_64, Linux 6.8, the sealed clean and uninitialized-read programs of
the Dockerfile's MSan check, run directly on the host (no seccomp), ASLR on:

  vm.mmap_rnd_bits=32, 40 runs each.  The previous version of this patch
  (reservation inside InitShadowWithReExec, allocator range open): clean 24
  passed, 16 SIGSEGV; uninitialized read 35 reported and aborted (134), 5
  SIGSEGV.  This version: clean 40 passed; uninitialized read 40 reported and
  aborted.  No SIGSEGV, no spin.
  vm.mmap_rnd_bits=28 (the host's setting), 40 runs each: 40 and 40 passed
  with either version.
  ulimit -s unlimited, 10 runs each: 10 and 10 passed.  This kernel places
  [vdso] beside the stack, not in the mmap area, so neither case spun here.

strace of a sealed run of a program that does malloc(8 MiB), memset and free:
after the start-up reservation no MAP_FIXED mapping lands in a shadow or origin
range.  The MAP_FIXED mappings left are the allocator's 4 TiB onto its own
reservation, together with 8 KiB at 0x740000000000 just above it for the
allocator's region metadata (upstream's TotalSpaceSize; not reserved here, since
it lies in an APP range); 128 KiB inside an 8 MiB PROT_NONE range the runtime
had reserved for itself; and musl's own brk guard page, which the uninstrumented
program makes too.  The freed block's shadow is handed back
with one madvise(MADV_DONTNEED) inside the shadow reservation.  Before
clear_shadow_mmap_threshold was pinned, the maintainers' trace of the same
program showed three 8 MiB MAP_FIXED mappings inside shadow-3.
---
 compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp       |  85 +++++++++++++++++-
 compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan_linux.cpp | 128 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 205 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp
index 161a8d708..a0dcc880b 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp
@@ -174,6 +174,69 @@ static void RegisterMsanFlags(FlagParser *parser, Flags *f) {
                           "deprecated, use halt_on_error");
 }
 
+// Sealed init must install no signal handler, must die by a signal on a
+// detection rather than by an exit status the program itself could have
+// returned, and must not re-map its shadow after start-up.  ASan pins the
+// common flags that decide these in asan_flags.cpp's SealFlags(); this pins the
+// same common flags for MSan, in MSan's own code, since sharing asan's would
+// rebuild the asan runtime.  asan's seal also pins protect_shadow_gap, an asan
+// flag with no MSan counterpart.  Pinned at the end of InitializeFlags, after
+// __msan_default_options() and MSAN_OPTIONS have been parsed, so a program's
+// own hook cannot reset it -- load-bearing for a consumer whose hook pins other
+// flags and must not unpin these.
+static void SealFlags() {
+  if (!SealedInit())
+    return;
+  // CopyFrom overwrites all of cf, so the pattern fill -ftrivial-auto-var-init
+  // would emit first is dead; leaving it out pays for the stores below.
+  [[clang::uninitialized]] CommonFlags cf;
+  cf.CopyFrom(*common_flags());
+  // No signal handler is installed and none is needed: a sealed detection dies
+  // by the abort below, which is a signal the program could not have chosen as
+  // an exit status.
+  cf.handle_segv = kHandleSignalNo;
+  cf.handle_sigbus = kHandleSignalNo;
+  cf.handle_abort = kHandleSignalNo;
+  cf.handle_sigill = kHandleSignalNo;
+  cf.handle_sigtrap = kHandleSignalNo;
+  cf.handle_sigfpe = kHandleSignalNo;
+  cf.use_sigaltstack = false;
+  cf.disable_coredump = false;
+  cf.detect_leaks = false;
+  // The RSS limit is enforced by a background thread; pinned off so a program
+  // hook cannot turn that thread on.
+  cf.hard_rss_limit_mb = 0;
+  cf.soft_rss_limit_mb = 0;
+  cf.heap_profile = false;
+  cf.allocator_release_to_os_interval_ms = -1;
+  // A detection dies by SIGABRT, not by an exit status the program could also
+  // have returned.
+  cf.abort_on_error = true;
+  // The shadow is zeroed with memset and never re-mapped -- asan's reason, and
+  // MSan's SetShadow takes the same flag.  Above this threshold SetShadow
+  // zero-fills a shadow range by mapping fresh pages over it with MAP_FIXED
+  // (MmapFixedSuperNoReserve) instead of writing zeros: on every unpoison of a
+  // large range, which is every large allocation and free and the unpoisoning
+  // of the main thread's sealed stack window at start-up.  In the model these
+  // binaries are verified against each of those is a new grant carved out of
+  // the shadow mapping, and the madvise(MADV_DONTNEED) that later hands a freed
+  // block's shadow back (patch 0020) may only cover a range inside ONE grant.
+  // With the threshold at its maximum the mappings SealedReserveShadow makes at
+  // start-up are the only shadow grants for the life of the process, so every
+  // madvise the runtime issues lies inside one of them.  The cost is a memset of
+  // the shadow where an mmap was: one shadow byte per byte unpoisoned (and one
+  // origin byte, when origins are tracked).
+  cf.clear_shadow_mmap_threshold = ~(uptr)0;
+  // Three flags asan's seal does not pin, all already off by default: the stack
+  // depot's background compression thread, printing the module map (a read of
+  // /proc/self/maps) and decorate_proc_maps (prctl names on mappings).  Pinned
+  // so that a hook cannot turn on a syscall the seal rules out.
+  cf.compress_stack_depot = 0;
+  cf.print_module_map = 0;
+  cf.decorate_proc_maps = false;
+  OverrideCommonFlags(cf);
+}
+
 static void InitializeFlags() {
   SetCommonFlagsDefaults();
   {
@@ -253,6 +316,8 @@ static void InitializeFlags() {
     Die();
   }
   if (f->store_context_size < 1) f->store_context_size = 1;
+
+  SealFlags();
 }
 
 void PrintWarningWithOrigin(uptr pc, uptr bp, u32 origin) {
@@ -487,6 +552,18 @@ void __msan_init() {
   uptr msan_init_frame;
   SealedSetInitFrame(reinterpret_cast<uptr>(&msan_init_frame));
 
+  // Sealed init reserves MSan's whole layout here -- shadow, origin, invalid
+  // and allocator ranges -- before the flag parser takes the runtime's first
+  // page, as AsanInitInternal reserves asan's shadow right after recording its
+  // frame.  Every mapping the runtime makes from this point on is placed by the
+  // kernel outside the reservation; reserved later, the runtime's own first
+  // mappings could land in the allocator's fixed range and be replaced by the
+  // allocator's MAP_FIXED.  __msan_get_track_origins() reads a link-time
+  // symbol, not a flag, so it is settled already.  The call to
+  // InitShadowWithReExec further down then does nothing.
+  if (SealedInit())
+    InitShadowWithReExec(__msan_get_track_origins());
+
   AvoidCVE_2016_2143();
 
   CacheBinaryName();
@@ -506,7 +583,13 @@ void __msan_init() {
   InstallAtExitHandler(); // Needs __cxa_atexit interceptor.
 
   DisableCoreDumperIfNecessary();
-  if (StackSizeIsUnlimited()) {
+  // Sealed init does not re-exec: GetArgsAndEnv returns empty vectors, so a
+  // ReExec would run /proc/self/exe with an empty argv and environment -- a
+  // start-up no ordinary run makes.  The sealed layout was reserved at the top
+  // of this function without it (see SealedReserveShadow), and with an
+  // unlimited stack the kernel's bottom-up layout falls in it, which the
+  // reservation refuses: the sealed spin, not a re-exec.
+  if (!SealedInit() && StackSizeIsUnlimited()) {
     VPrintf(1, "Unlimited stack, doing reexec\n");
     // A reasonably large stack size. It is bigger than the usual 8Mb, because,
     // well, the program could have been run with unlimited stack for a reason.
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan_linux.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan_linux.cpp
index 7140de7e9..1eb71b712 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan_linux.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan_linux.cpp
@@ -34,9 +34,18 @@
 #  include "msan_report.h"
 #  include "msan_thread.h"
 #  include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_common.h"
+#  include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix.h"
 #  include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_procmaps.h"
+#  include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h"
 #  include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_stackdepot.h"
 
+#  if SANITIZER_LINUX
+#    include <sys/mman.h>
+#    ifndef MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE
+#      define MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE 0x100000
+#    endif
+#  endif
+
 namespace __msan {
 
 void ReportMapRange(const char *descr, uptr beg, uptr size) {
@@ -112,6 +121,27 @@ static void CheckMemoryLayoutSanity() {
   }
 }
 
+// What start-up does with one range of kMemoryLayout: a SHADOW range, and an
+// ORIGIN range when origins are tracked, is mapped readable-writable; an
+// INVALID range, and an ORIGIN range when they are not, is protected; APP and
+// ALLOCATOR ranges are neither.  One rule for InitShadow and for the sealed
+// reservation below, so that the two cannot disagree.
+enum class RangeAction { kNone, kMap, kProtect };
+
+static RangeAction LayoutRangeAction(MappingDesc::Type type, bool init_origins) {
+  bool map = type == MappingDesc::SHADOW ||
+             (init_origins && type == MappingDesc::ORIGIN);
+  bool protect = type == MappingDesc::INVALID ||
+                 (!init_origins && type == MappingDesc::ORIGIN);
+  CHECK(!(map && protect));
+  if (map)
+    return RangeAction::kMap;
+  if (protect)
+    return RangeAction::kProtect;
+  CHECK(type == MappingDesc::APP || type == MappingDesc::ALLOCATOR);
+  return RangeAction::kNone;
+}
+
 static bool InitShadow(bool init_origins, bool dry_run) {
   // Let user know mapping parameters first.
   VPrintf(1, "__msan_init %p\n", reinterpret_cast<void *>(&__msan_init));
@@ -140,14 +170,10 @@ static bool InitShadow(bool init_origins, bool dry_run) {
     if (start >= maxVirtualAddress)
       continue;
 
-    bool map = type == MappingDesc::SHADOW ||
-               (init_origins && type == MappingDesc::ORIGIN);
-    bool protect = type == MappingDesc::INVALID ||
-                   (!init_origins && type == MappingDesc::ORIGIN);
-    CHECK(!(map && protect));
+    const RangeAction action = LayoutRangeAction(type, init_origins);
+    bool map = action == RangeAction::kMap;
+    bool protect = action == RangeAction::kProtect;
     if (!map && !protect) {
-      CHECK(type == MappingDesc::APP || type == MappingDesc::ALLOCATOR);
-
       if (dry_run && type == MappingDesc::ALLOCATOR &&
           !CheckMemoryRangeAvailability(start, size, !dry_run))
         return false;
@@ -172,7 +198,95 @@ static bool InitShadow(bool init_origins, bool dry_run) {
   return true;
 }
 
+#  if SANITIZER_LINUX
+// One range of the sealed layout, mapped with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE.  Mirrors
+// asan_shadow_setup.cpp's SealedReserveRange.
+static void SealedReserveRange(uptr beg, uptr size, int prot) {
+  if (size == 0)
+    return;
+  uptr res = internal_mmap(
+      reinterpret_cast<void *>(beg), size, prot,
+      MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_NORESERVE | MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, -1, 0);
+  // Anything but the range itself -- an errno (EEXIST: a mapping already there;
+  // ENOMEM: the layout does not fit), or a placement elsewhere by a kernel that
+  // predates the flag and read it as a hint -- is the runtime failing to get
+  // memory for itself at start-up.  Take the one path that has: spin, making no
+  // syscall.  See SealedHangOnAllocationFailure.
+  if (res != beg)
+    SealedHangOnAllocationFailure();
+  DecreaseTotalMmap(size);  // Not counted against mmap_limit_mb, as ever.
+}
+
+// Set once the sealed layout is reserved.  __msan_init reserves it first thing
+// and calls InitShadowWithReExec again later, the way an unsealed run does;
+// that second call finds this set and does nothing.
+static bool sealed_layout_reserved;
+
+// The sealed replacement for InitShadow's map/protect loop, called from the
+// top of __msan_init, before the runtime's first allocation.  The unsealed
+// path asks MemoryRangeIsAvailable -- a /proc read -- and then MAP_FIXEDs, but
+// in sealed mode that question answers yes unasked (patch 0012), so the dry run
+// cannot fail and the MAP_FIXED that follows would cover whatever the program
+// or the kernel had already placed in a shadow range.  The specification these
+// binaries are verified against admits every such placement, so the runtime
+// reserves instead, with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, which the kernel refuses where
+// MAP_FIXED would have replaced, and spins on refusal.  Shadow and
+// mapped-origin ranges are reserved readable-writable, invalid and
+// unmapped-origin ranges PROT_NONE -- the disposition InitShadow gives each
+// (LayoutRangeAction), with one more mmap flag.
+//
+// The ALLOCATOR range is reserved too, PROT_NONE.  Unlike asan's, MSan's
+// primary allocator has a fixed base (kAllocatorSpace in msan_allocator.cpp,
+// duplicated in kMemoryLayout), which it maps with MAP_FIXED.  Left open, a
+// mapping the kernel placed there -- the [vdso] and [vvar] it places at exec,
+// or any unhinted mapping the runtime makes before the allocator starts --
+// would be replaced by that MAP_FIXED without a word.  Reserved, such a mapping is a refusal here (the spin), and
+// the allocator's MAP_FIXED later lands on the runtime's own reservation.  APP
+// ranges are left alone: the program is already in them.
+//
+// No madvise: the sealed shadow is neither MADV_DONTDUMP nor hinted
+// (sanitizer_sealed.h).
+static void SealedReserveShadow(bool init_origins) {
+  CheckMemoryLayoutSanity();
+  const uptr maxVirtualAddress = GetMaxUserVirtualAddress();
+  for (unsigned i = 0; i < kMemoryLayoutSize; ++i) {
+    uptr start = kMemoryLayout[i].start;
+    uptr end = kMemoryLayout[i].end;
+    uptr size = end - start;
+    MappingDesc::Type type = kMemoryLayout[i].type;
+    if (start >= maxVirtualAddress)
+      continue;
+    switch (LayoutRangeAction(type, init_origins)) {
+      case RangeAction::kMap:
+        SealedReserveRange(start, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE);
+        break;
+      case RangeAction::kProtect:
+        SealedReserveRange(start, size, PROT_NONE);
+        break;
+      case RangeAction::kNone:
+        if (type == MappingDesc::ALLOCATOR)
+          SealedReserveRange(start, size, PROT_NONE);
+        break;
+    }
+  }
+  sealed_layout_reserved = true;
+}
+#  endif  // SANITIZER_LINUX
+
 bool InitShadowWithReExec(bool init_origins) {
+#  if SANITIZER_LINUX
+  // A sealed run reserves its layout with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE and never
+  // re-execs: its MemoryRangeIsAvailable answers yes unasked, so the dry run
+  // below could neither fail nor justify a re-exec, and the re-exec would run
+  // /proc/self/exe with the empty argv and environment sealed mode returns.
+  // The first call, from the top of __msan_init, reserves; any later one finds
+  // the layout reserved and does nothing.
+  if (SealedInit()) {
+    if (!sealed_layout_reserved)
+      SealedReserveShadow(init_origins);
+    return true;
+  }
+#  endif
   // Start with dry run: check layout is ok, but don't print warnings because
   // warning messages will cause tests to fail (even if we successfully re-exec
   // after the warning).
